    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe structure fits this codebase's established conventions cleanly and additively:\n\n- **`OwnerPrimaryIdentifier` matches a strong existing idiom.** The `rest/function/owner` package already contains a family of `public final` single-source-of-truth helpers with private constructors and \"Single source of truth for an owner's X\" Javadoc \u2014 `OwnerCustomerCode`, `OwnerIdentity`, `OwnerTelephone`, `OwnerEmail`, `OwnerSoundex`, `OwnerRegion`. The new class follows that template exactly (same package, `final` + private ctor, same Javadoc phrasing, static accessor). A maintainer would find it entirely natural, not a novel abstraction. It reads the persisted `customerCode` field (consistent with how an already-stored owner is treated elsewhere, e.g. `OwnerIdentity.identityKey(Owner)`).\n\n- **The new class is the right unit for the spec's forward-looking requirement.** The spec explicitly calls out the future customerCode\u2192memberId migration and asks that the event carry \"whatever replaces it later.\" Centralizing that one decision behind `OwnerPrimaryIdentifier.of()` is exactly the seam the spec motivates, and it isolates the future change to one place \u2014 matching the \"only this method changes; every consumer carries the new identifier\" intent. For a single consumer this would normally risk premature abstraction, but the spec's named future migration plus the package's existing pattern justify it.\n\n- **`AuditOwnerCreated` stays cohesive.** Emitting a second, structured audit record is a natural extension of a class whose sole job is auditing owner creation; it isn't becoming a catch-all. The `SEQ`/`JSON` statics are appropriately scoped to this responsibility.\n\n- **No meaningful duplication.** The human-readable line deliberately logs the literal `customerCode` field directly, while the structured event's identifier slot goes through the abstraction. That distinction (literal field vs. \"primary identifier\") is intentional and correct, not a copy.\n\nOne thing I noted but do not consider a structural defect: the JSON key stays literally `\"customerCode\"` even though it's sourced from the migration-aware helper, so after the memberId unification the field name and value will diverge in meaning. That field name is dictated by the spec, so it's out of scope for a structure review \u2014 flagging only for awareness.",
